course: dictionary
lesson: 3353
title: Strong words: exceedingly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words make other words stronger. «Exceedingly» is one of them: it tells you how much. Look for it in the words around it, and ask how strong the feeling or quality is.
explain: «Now, the very next time this happens, I shall be exceedingly angry.» The Badger is not just a bit cross. Exceedingly means very or extremely, so he is very angry indeed. Check by swapping in very: the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Exceedingly is a stiff, polite, old-fashioned way of saying «very much». You will meet it in stories and formal writing more than in playground talk. In everyday speech, say «really» or «super» instead.
fact: Exceedingly is an adverb: it tells you how much, and it works with adjectives like angry, kind and rare.
fact: «Exceedingly» means very or extremely.
fact: Phrases you may meet: exceedingly rare, exceedingly well, exceedingly difficult, exceedingly grateful.
fact: In science, «exceedingly small» means much smaller than you can see.
fact: When you read «exceedingly», the author is using a formal or old-fashioned way of saying «very much».
exercise: «Now, the very next time this happens, I shall be exceedingly angry.» What does exceedingly mean here? | Not at all | A little bit | *Very or extremely | Slowly
exercise: Ella says, «That was exceedingly kind of you.» Which everyday word could you use instead of exceedingly? | Quietly | Lazily | Sadly | *Really
exercise: Which word in «exceedingly rare» is the adverb? | *exceedingly | neither word | both words | rare
test: «The soup was exceedingly hot.» What does exceedingly mean in this sentence? | Very cold | Slightly | Never | *Extremely
test: Which word is the stiff, old-fashioned way of saying «very much»? | Really | Totally | *Exceedingly | Super
exam: «Thank you, I am exceedingly grateful for your help.» Which word means about the same as exceedingly here? | Sadly | *Very | Barely | Quickly
